\section{Which terms are listed}{

\itemize{
\item A numeric attribute gets \code{ego()}, \code{alter()}, \code{diff()}, and \code{sim()},
and a categorical one \code{ego()}, \code{alter()}, \code{same()}, and \code{mix()}.
A name, a label, or an attribute with a different category for each
node gets no term.
\item An undirected network has no \code{alter()}, \code{exchange()}, \code{outdeg()}, or
\code{recip}, since the two sides of its ties are the same.
\item A two-mode network reads \code{ego()} on the first mode and \code{alter()} on
the second. A term of both sides needs the attribute on both modes.
\item The reporter terms are only for a cognitive social structure, and
\code{global()} only for a list of networks. An MRQAP takes \code{inertia} and
\code{trans} only on a panel.
\item A term that the model drops or refuses, such as \code{tie()} of the outcome
layer, has no row. Nor has a term that reads the outcome's own ties,
such as \code{recip} in an MRQAP of one network, since permuting the
outcome does not permute the term with it.
}

The terms are for the outcome on the left-hand side, by default \code{.}.
Where \code{.} is not one layer, as in a multiplex network without a focal
layer, the terms are for its first layer, and a message says so.
